\section{Primary BRMM source and bounded-bias motion theorem}
\label{sec:brmm-source}

BRMM (bounded recurrent marine motion) is the physical source hypothesis
for the primary practical-motion theorem. A spectral or vessel-response model
can provide an example of physical motion, but membership in such a model
does not define BRMM admission. One physical
root and continuation produce $a_m,\omega,R$, frontend and geometry states,
committed $\tau,\sigma_{aw},R_S,T_S$, and the actual covariance/event history.
There are no independent acceleration, bias, tuner or pseudo-measurement boxes.

\subsection{BRMM physical motion: no acceleration DC in either branch}

Let $a_m(t)\in\mathbb R^3$ be non-gravitational CoG acceleration in one
fixed physical world frame. On the admitted Normal-Live execution require
\[
 \|a_m(t)\|\le4.0\ {\rm m\,s^{-2}},\qquad
 \|\omega(t)\|\le30^\circ/{\rm s}.
\]
Thus $5.80665\le\|a_m-g\|\le13.80665\ {\rm m\,s^{-2}}$ for standard
gravity. Positive sea-height cases satisfy $0<H_s\le8.5\ {\rm m}$;
zero motion is also admitted. At $H_s=0$ use fixed physical coordinate
scales, never division by sea height. These caps are admission premises,
not assertions that every reference replay satisfies them.

\paragraph{BRMM-0: prohibit physical acceleration DC.}
For the entire admitted continuation, require an absolutely continuous
same-history velocity primitive $v_m$ and a uniform finite constant $V_m$:
\begin{equation}
 a_m=\dot v_m\quad\hbox{a.e.},\qquad
 \sup_{t\ge t_{\rm Live}}\|v_m(t)\|\le V_m<\infty.
 \label{eq:brmm-zero-dc}
\end{equation}
This applies to both Q and O and every transition between them. For every
$t$ and $T>0$ within that continuation, the fundamental theorem of calculus gives
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\frac1T\int_t^{t+T}a_m(s)\,ds\right\|
 \le\frac{2V_m}{T}\longrightarrow0
 \quad\hbox{uniformly in }t.
 \label{eq:brmm-zero-mean}
\end{equation}
In particular, $a_m(t)\equiv d\ne0$ is prohibited: its primitive grows as
$td$. A persistent nonzero long-time mean hidden inside oscillatory
acceleration is also prohibited. Finite transient impulses and zero motion
remain admissible. A fixed-window bound alone, however small, does not
exclude all constant accelerations; nor does small AC energy.

For example, a finite harmonic acceleration with fixed vector coefficients,
\begin{equation}
 a_m(t)=\sum_i\bigl[A_i\cos(\omega_i t)+B_i\sin(\omega_i t)\bigr],
 \qquad \omega_i>0,
\end{equation}
has the same-history primitive
\begin{equation}
 v_m(t)=v_0+\sum_i\frac{A_i\sin(\omega_i t)-B_i\cos(\omega_i t)}{\omega_i}.
\end{equation}
It satisfies BRMM-0 for all time with
$V_m=\|v_0\|+\sum_i(\|A_i\|+\|B_i\|)/\omega_i$.
A constant velocity drift changes this bound but contributes no acceleration
DC. Uniformity over a family requires a uniform bound on this expression.
This analytical implication does not by itself certify a replay's exact
physical attachment, the acceleration/rate caps, or O-branch return lobes.

Constant measurement offset belongs to BIAS0, not to $a_m$. Subtracting a
sample mean cannot manufacture physical BRMM membership: the same actual
acceleration must drive the source and observer. Sensor bias, compensation
residuals and model mismatch retain their own input budgets. Bounded
velocity alone does not bound displacement or the $S$ primitive. Bounds on
these additional same-history primitives, or their physical forcing
energies, remain explicit obligations. The actual $S=0$ residual
$\delta S-S_{\rm true}$ and its applied anisotropic $R_S$ remain in the graph.

\subsection{Window recurrence and quiet motion}

For every start time $t_k$ and common duration $T_R>0$, put
\[
 \bar a_k=T_R^{-1}\int_{t_k}^{t_k+T_R}a_m(t)\,dt,\qquad
 E_k=\int_{t_k}^{t_k+T_R}\|a_m(t)-\bar a_k\|^2\,dt.
\]
Require the finite-window mean-reversion bound on \emph{both} branches,
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\int_{t_k}^{t_k+T_R}a_m(t)\,dt\right\|\le V_R.
 \label{eq:brmm-window-impulse}
\end{equation}
One may take $V_R=2V_m$ from BRMM-0; a smaller bound needs separate evidence.
Every window satisfies one of
\[
 {\rm Q}:\ E_k\le E_q,\qquad
 {\rm O}:\ E_k>E_q,\quad E_k\ge E_{\min},
 \qquad E_q\ge0,\quad E_{\min}>0.
\]
Only O additionally requires disjoint subintervals $I_k^+,I_k^-$ with
impulses $j_k^\pm=\int_{I_k^\pm}a_m(t)\,dt$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \|j_k^\pm\|\ge J_{\min}>0,\qquad
 (j_k^+)^\top j_k^-\le-\chi J_{\min}^2,\qquad\chi>0.
 \label{eq:brmm-opposing-lobes}
\end{equation}
No minimum wave amplitude or O-branch excitation is imposed on Q. Quiet
does not mean DC: Q still obeys BRMM-0 and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-window-impulse}. An optional bound
$\mathrm{TV}_{[t_k,t_k+T_R]}(a_m)\le V_a$ may be added if a concrete
finite-map enclosure needs it; it is not currently a frozen premise.

The initial audit uses $T_R=10\,{\rm s}$, quiet RMS thresholds
$0.03$ and $0.05\,{\rm m\,s^{-2}}$ (respectively
$E_q=0.009$ and $0.025\,{\rm m^2\,s^{-3}}$), and candidate
$V_R=2\,{\rm m\,s^{-1}}$. These are diagnostic choices, not qualified
uniform constants. $T_R,E_q,V_R,E_{\min},J_{\min},\chi,V_a$ and the primitive
bounds remain to be assigned with evidence.
The proposed outer tuner interval $[0.02,1.2]\,{\rm Hz}$ does not change the
shipping defaults $[0.03,1.2]\,{\rm Hz}$ and is not a physical motion
bandlimit. A $0.02\,{\rm Hz}$ sinusoid has a $50\,{\rm s}$ period:
opposing lobes in every $10\,{\rm s}$ window are not automatic.

\paragraph{Certificate entry and audit limits.}
The eight v1.2.1 vessel-response JONSWAP/PM--Stokes records at
$H_s\in\{0.27,1.5,4,8.5\}\,{\rm m}$ are tested on every sample-aligned
$10\,{\rm s}$ window. Outward arithmetic encloses binary64 sample values
under an explicit zero-order-hold interpretation. Stored lobe intervals
certify positive witnesses; a finite search finding none leaves the window
unresolved. Finite sampled statistics neither prove infinite-time BRMM-0
nor enclose physical intersample motion, all continuous window offsets,
Normal-Live entry, or the complete source family. They suggest candidate
constants and expose sampled-envelope violations; they do not set theorem constants.

The sampled v1.2.1 audit contains 1,920,000 samples and 1,904,008 complete
sample-aligned windows. All windows fall in the diagnostic O branch.
There are 10,205 samples above the acceleration cap and 31,937 above
the rate cap. Restricting to the runtime Live flag leaves 10,002 and
31,589 such samples. Moreover, the runtime covariance settings differ
from the configured vector-covariance premises used by the conditional
matrix certificate. No sampled execution satisfies all audited admission
checks, even where the physical caps hold. The trace observer is bitwise
transparent to ordinary replay output; two of eight replays fail their
unchanged performance gates. None of these finite-record observations
promotes source admission, P4 or P5.

\paragraph{Proof obligations under the replacement source.}
BIAS0/1 supplies qualified true-bias bounds and its connected history;
estimate projection supplies compactness, not bias-error decay.
P1/P2 must attach each BRMM history to the exact shipping Normal-Live graph
(lever arm disabled, vibration guard transparent, all valid accelerometer
samples accepted, all asynchronous vector events, process floors, resets
and every due actual-$R_S$ correction). P3 retains $\delta=10^{-18}$ and its
matrix implications only where their actual premises are established.
Scalar BRMM recurrence does not prove vector persistent excitation,
especially in Q. The conditional P3 implication below covers Q, O and their
mixtures through the same execution premises in both modes. A source
declaration or sampled audit does not establish those premises.

\paragraph{What Normal-Live means for P3.}
It is an execution regime, not merely the runtime \texttt{Live} flag.
Write $\mathcal C_W$ for the existing explicit hypotheses used by the
matrix certificates: the source-generated Live covariance and shipping
held-to-active release; accepted accelerometer updates at every valid IMU
sample; the declared asynchronous vector-PE occurrences with one-second
recurrence, magnetic norm $10$--$200\,\mu{\rm T}$, vector sine separation at
least $0.1$, the stated specific-force bounds and body-rate cap; committed
OU and scheduler invariants; full configured process and measurement
covariances, PSD latent-acceleration floors, every due actual-$R_S$ update,
and each immediate covariance reset. The lever arm is disabled, the
vibration guard is dormant/transparent, required factorizations succeed,
and hard attitude rewrites are separate hybrid entries. Geometry bounds
must describe the actual Jacobians on that same history.

Some parts of $\mathcal C_W$ are shipping identities or clamp invariants;
accepted-vector recurrence, geometry and branch retention are execution
assumptions requiring separate admission evidence. Scalar BRMM recurrence
and the runtime Live flag do not imply them. In particular, no inequality
$\Omega_W\succeq\delta P_W$, nonlinear contraction, or bias-error decay is
assumed in $\mathcal C_W$.

The conditional P3 claim is
\[
 (\mathrm{BRMM}\ \text{and}\ \mathcal C_W)
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \Omega_W-10^{-18}P_W\succeq0 .
\]
Its numerical chain uses the complete H18 information word and following
prediction, outward full-matrix prior-free completion, then the exact
held-to-active covariance bridge and event preservation. Those matrix
steps use no spectral membership or positive lower bound on wave energy.
Thus the same implication applies to Q, O and mixed histories satisfying
$\mathcal C_W$; this is not a claim that all BRMM histories satisfy it.
Physical source admission and P4 are separate.

The shipping bias projection changes the nominal bias but does not modify
$P$. The $P,\Psi,\Omega$ covariance comparison is consequently unchanged
by that event, including at the closed $0.4\,{\rm m\,s^{-2}}$ boundary.
Here $\Psi$ denotes the formal covariance-word transport, not the
derivative of the projected nonlinear error map.
Subsequent Jacobians and events must still satisfy $\mathcal C_W$. This
covariance identity neither bounds the nonlinear projection/reset error
map nor proves subsequent domain retention. The finite configured bias
correlation used in the A21 covariance bridge is not a conclusion that
physical bias error decays.

For P4, primitive and recurrence constraints enter the same full-history
graph and source/forcing forms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-master}.
Where BIAS2 sharpens a gain, its positive sector must be proved on this
actual graph, not inferred from oscillatory source energy or bias compactness.
First check the attached finite-word motion master with useful channel gains.
Only a feasible quantitative endpoint and retention margin justifies outward
source coverage. Then certify every-prefix finite gain and first-exit
retention, including the closed projection boundary, before P5 capture.
Neither a sampled recurrence audit nor an unusably large finite input gain
promotes P4.

\subsection{Primary target: bounded bias and practical motion stability}
\label{sec:brmm-bounded-bias-motion}

Write the exact finite error as $(e_H,e_b)$, where
\begin{equation}
 e_H=(c,\delta b_g,\delta v,\delta p,\delta S,\delta a_w)\in\mathbb R^{18},
 \qquad e_b=b_a^{\rm true}-\hat b_a\in\mathbb R^3.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-coordinates}
\end{equation}
Here $c$ is the finite Cayley coordinate, not a small-angle substitution.
In H18 the estimate is held but the physical bias continues; in A21 both
coordinates follow the full prediction/correction/projection recurrence of
BIAS1. Selectivity of the \emph{storage} does not remove states from the filter.
Choose a same-source storage, in fixed physically scaled coordinates,
\begin{equation}
 W_H(e_H,\zeta)=e_H^\top M_H(\zeta)e_H,\qquad
 m_-\|e_H\|^2\le W_H\le m_+\|e_H\|^2,
 \quad 0<m_-\le m_+<\infty.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-storage}
\end{equation}
In active mode $\zeta$ retains the complete 21-state nominal/covariance and
bias history. The active gains are not the held-mode gains: for example the
accelerometer gain numerator for $e_H$ contains
$P_{HH}H_H^\top+P_{Hb}$, and its innovation covariance includes the bias
and cross blocks. Thus the H18 certificate cannot be copied to active mode,
nor may a Schur/reduced-state replacement estimator be used.

\begin{lemma}[Conditional compactness of accelerometer-bias error]
\label{lem:brmm-bias-compactness}
Assume the initial estimate lies in the deployed projection ball of radius
$R_b=0.4\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. Hold, finite-$\tau_b$ prediction with factor in
$(0,1]$, norm-preserving frame changes and the projection after every state
injection preserve $\|\hat b_a\|\le R_b$. If BIAS0/1 additionally supplies a
same-history hard envelope $\|b_a^{\rm true}\|\le B_{\rm true}$, then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\|e_b\|\le B_e:=B_{\rm true}+R_b.}
 \label{eq:brmm-bias-compact-error}
\end{equation}
No convergence of $e_b$ or identification of $b_a^{\rm true}$ is asserted.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each listed estimate operation preserves the ball, so induction gives the
estimate bound. The triangle inequality gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-bias-compact-error}.
The argument does not require true bias to lie inside the projection ball.
\end{proof}
The physical envelope includes uncompensated deterministic offsets and the
BIAS1 driving convolution. A stochastic OU PSD is not a deterministic cap:
a finite-horizon high-probability envelope gives only a correspondingly
conditional finite-horizon conclusion, not an almost-sure infinite-time bound.
Assembled-sensor qualification is required for deployment, not for proving
this conditional implication with symbolic or declared physical bounds.
The estimate bound concerns committed states after the immediate projection;
the pre-projection correction is an auxiliary graph coordinate and need not
lie in the ball.

For this weaker target the old $0.35$ active-estimate interior premise is
replaced by the closed $R_b$ ball; the remaining physical BRMM, event,
lever-arm and dormant-vibration premises are retained. Consequently all
projection-boundary branches must be covered. An interior-only certificate,
cannot be silently extended to the boundary. P3 uses the covariance-only
projection identity above, whereas P4 still needs the finite nonlinear
projection sector and retention proof. The deployed filter is unchanged.

On a complete word define the \emph{internal coupling energy}
\begin{equation}
 D_{b,k}=\sum_{j=k}^{k+N_W-1}h_j\|e_{b,j}\|^2
 \le T_W B_e^2,\qquad T_W=\sum_{j=k}^{k+N_W-1}h_j.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-bias-energy}
\end{equation}
Fix $e_{b,j}$ at the sample entrance; every intra-sample bias correction and
projection remains explicitly in the exact joint graph. A certificate must
bound their effect with that convention; it cannot silently sample a different
bias history or drop a correction. $D_b$ is not an exogenous noise budget:
it bounds the actual internal bias-error trajectory, without assuming it is
independent of $e_H$. BIAS0/1 supplies its root/forcing dependence. A BIAS2
sector can sharpen the coupling gain, but positive separation is not required
merely to assert bias compactness; any sector used still needs its own valid
same-history, same-prefix proof.

The new P4-motion obligations, for both Q/O branches, their admissible mixtures, and both actual
filter modes, are
\begin{align}
 W_{H,k+N_W}&\le\rho_H W_{H,k}
       +\gamma_bD_{b,k}+\gamma_sD_{s,k}+\gamma_nD_{n,k},
       &&0<\rho_H<1, \label{eq:brmm-motion-endpoint}\\
 W_{H,k;\ell}&\le\Gamma W_{H,k}
       +\kappa_bD_{b,k}+\kappa_sD_{s,k}+\kappa_nD_{n,k},
       &&\ell\in\operatorname{Pref}(W),\quad 1\le\Gamma<\infty.
       \label{eq:brmm-motion-prefix}
\end{align}
All gains are finite and nonnegative. Prefix contraction is not required.
Here $\operatorname{Pref}(W)$ includes every completed shipping event, not
only IMU sample boundaries; each correction includes its immediate reset and
projection, with their auxiliary states retained in the graph.
Every physical model, measurement and gyro-discretization defect stays in its
declared source/noise channel. Removing sensor noise does not set $D_s=0$.
In particular, ocean-wave truth generally does not obey the filter's latent
OU prediction: $x^{\rm true}_{L,j+1}-F_{LL,j}x^{\rm true}_{L,j}$ can remain
nonzero in a noise-free run. Its budget is not removed to obtain contraction.

For a common augmented graph coordinate $z$, write
$e_{H,0}=C_0z$, $e_{H,N}=C_Nz$, and
$D_i=z^\top Q_i z$ for $i\in\{b,s,n\}$. The endpoint master is
\begin{equation}
 L_H=C_N^\top M_{H,N}C_N-\rho_H C_0^\top M_{H,0}C_0
       -\gamma_bQ_b-\gamma_sQ_s-\gamma_nQ_n.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-master}
\end{equation}
The maps and all dense cross terms come from the same full shipping graph,
including every actual-$R_S$ S event and its finite reset. Valid graph sectors
$z^\top\Pi_jz\ge0$ and $\lambda_j\ge0$ with
$-(L_H+\sum_j\lambda_j\Pi_j)\succ0$ suffice. This is a sufficient augmented
test, not a claim that every sound certificate must be strictly definite in
redundant graph coordinates. Prefix masters replace $C_N,M_{H,N},\rho_H$
by the corresponding prefix maps/metric and $\Gamma$, using only valid prefix
sectors. This changes the performance storage, not the executed covariance.

\begin{lemma}[Finite-coefficient realization of the motion supply test]
\label{lem:brmm-motion-finite-coefficients}
Suppose the exact completed-event error graph has a factorization
\begin{equation}
 e_{j+1}=A_j(e,\zeta)e_j+B_j(e,\zeta)u_j,\qquad e_j\in\mathbb R^{21}.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-finite-coefficients}
\end{equation}
The coefficient sequence is evaluated on the same complete error/source
history, including actual gains and finite resets. Let $C_H$ and $C_b$
select the 18 performance and three bias-error coordinates. Fix a positive
factor $a$, bias gain $g_b>0$, and positive definite port costs $U_j$ in
explicitly declared units. Charge $h_jg_b\|C_b e_j\|^2$ at sample entrances,
including the root; do not charge it again at each correction. For a fixed
coefficient sequence, define the auxiliary inverse minimum-energy matrix
\begin{align}
 X_0&=(aC_H^\top M_{H,0}C_H+h_0g_bC_b^\top C_b)^{-1},\nonumber\\
 X_j&\leftarrow(X_j^{-1}+h_jg_bC_b^\top C_b)^{-1}
       &&\text{at later sample entrances},\nonumber\\
 X_{j+1}&=A_jX_jA_j^\top+B_jU_j^{-1}B_j^\top.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-supply-elimination}
\end{align}
Assume these matrices are positive definite. If
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_{\max}\!\left(L_j^\top C_HX_jC_H^\top L_j\right)\le1,
 \qquad M_{H,j}=L_jL_j^\top,
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-supply-eigenvalue}
\end{equation}
then $W_{H,j}\le aW_{H,0}+g_bD_{b,0:j}+\sum_{i<j}u_i^\top U_i u_i$
on that coefficient sequence. Uniform validity over all coefficient
sequences generated by the retained nonlinear BRMM graph gives the
corresponding finite-error inequality. Use $a<1$ for the endpoint and
$a=\Gamma\ge1$ for every completed-event prefix.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For fixed coefficients, minimize the positive quadratic input cost subject
to the full linear equality graph in Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-finite-coefficients}.
At an entrance the bias cost adds to its Hessian. Eliminating the forcing
coordinates through one transition gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-supply-elimination}; the minimum cost at $e_j$ is
$e_j^\top X_j^{-1}e_j$. Equation~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-supply-eigenvalue}
is equivalent to $C_H^\top M_{H,j}C_H\preceq X_j^{-1}$.
The actual history has at least this minimum cost. Applying the inequality
to each admissible dependent coefficient sequence proves the stated claim.
No bias-error decay is used.
\end{proof}

The auxiliary $X_j$ is a quadratic-cost elimination variable, not the filter's
covariance or another estimator. All 21 state coordinates and cross terms
remain. Exact finite coefficients can use
$(E(c)-I)f=-(I-[c]_\times/2)^{-1}[f]_\times c$ and, with
$\widetilde d=\operatorname{Cayley}(R_{\rm shipping}(d))$,
$c^+=(c-\widetilde d-\tfrac12c\times\widetilde d)/
(1+\tfrac14c^\top\widetilde d)$, on their retained charts. These are finite
identities, not differential approximations. Physical $S$ forcing remains
$u_S=-S_{\rm true}$ alongside the actual $R_S$ correction.

A numerical check with coefficients frozen on one attached word does not
establish their uniform nonlinear/source dependence. Allowing arbitrary
energy ports for that sufficient check does not replace BRMM with independent
sample boxes; a failing maximizing port history need not be physically
admissible. A successful point check still needs outward uniform coverage,
the declared bias/source budgets, and the retention test below. In particular,
large finite gains alone do not certify a useful accuracy floor.

\begin{theorem}[Regional practical motion stability with compact bias error]
\label{thm:brmm-bounded-bias-motion}
Assume the bias compactness lemma and complete same-history source/event
coverage above, uniform metric bounds, and a finite word length $T_W\le\bar T$.
Retain the canonical P3 matrix checks at $\delta=10^{-18}$, but separately
establish their applicable source premises and the active-mode motion gain;
P3 alone does not prove Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-endpoint}. Suppose P4-motion
proves Eqs.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-endpoint}--\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-prefix}
on the relevant first-exit graph and source/noise windows satisfy
$D_s\le\bar D_s$, $D_n\le\bar D_n$. Put
\begin{align}
 C&=\gamma_b\bar T B_e^2+\gamma_s\bar D_s+\gamma_n\bar D_n,\nonumber\\
 C_p&=\kappa_b\bar T B_e^2+\kappa_s\bar D_s+\kappa_n\bar D_n.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-budgets}
\end{align}
Suppose an endpoint level $L$ and chart-valid level $L_{\rm chart}$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
 W_{H,k_c}\le L,\qquad \rho_H L+C\le L,\qquad
 \Gamma L+C_p<L_{\rm chart}.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-retention}
\end{equation}
All non-energy source/bias domains are also retained at every subevent.
Then, on successive same-mode complete words after entry,
\begin{align}
 W_{H,k_c+mN_W}&\le\rho_H^m W_{H,k_c}
                   +\frac{1-\rho_H^m}{1-\rho_H}C,\nonumber\\
 \limsup_k W_{H,k}&\le\Gamma\frac{C}{1-\rho_H}+C_p,
 \qquad \|e_{b,k}\|\le B_e.
 \label{eq:brmm-motion-ultimate}
\end{align}
The infinite-time statement requires the envelopes on all successive windows.
A full startup-to-active result additionally requires P5-motion finite capture
and explicit metric/error/source transport at H$\to$A and all other allowed
hybrid events. Capture cannot be inferred from bias compactness.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The bias lemma bounds each internal coupling energy. Iteration of the endpoint
inequality gives the geometric-series bound. The two level inequalities and
the independently checked source domains exclude a first exit, so the graph
premises remain valid without circularly assuming retention. Apply the prefix
inequality and take the limsup to obtain Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-ultimate}.
Metric coercivity converts this into a motion-error norm bound. A certified
hybrid transport restarts the argument at the next admitted mode level.
\end{proof}

The useful performance quantity is the resulting error floor, in particular
the bias contribution
\[
 \frac{\Gamma\gamma_b\bar T B_e^2}{1-\rho_H}+\kappa_b\bar T B_e^2,
\]
not compactness alone. It must satisfy the desired
motion-accuracy tolerances as well as chart retention. No numerical gain,
accuracy floor, source-uniform motion certificate or bias convergence is
claimed here. P4-motion/P5-motion are separate gates from the retained stronger
full-state P4/P5 gates.
Even with $\bar D_n=0$ and vanishing bias error, nonzero $\bar D_s$ generally
leaves a positive certified upper error floor. The theorem does not require
that floor to be zero, nor does it claim that the upper bound is the attained
error. Observed percentage errors are performance evidence, not numerical
theorem constants until the relevant output normalization, source family and
gain bound have been certified.
